 "standard_url": "https://www.education.gov.za/Portals/0/CD/National%20Curriculum%20Statements%20and%20Vocational/CAPS%20MATHS%20%20ENGLISH%20GR%201-3%20FS.pdf",
 "standard_text": "Solve and explain solutions to practical problems that involve equal sharing and grouping with whole numbers up to 50 and with answers that can include remainders; division of whole numbers.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 2 maths worksheets: sharing equally, grouping, division facts and remainders. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "15 ÷ 5 = 3 because 3 × 5 = 15.",
 "key_sub": "Say what is left over.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Sharing: give one each in turn until all are gone.</li><li>Grouping: make groups of the same size and count the groups.</li><li>15 ÷ 5 = 3 because 3 × 5 = 15.</li><li>What cannot be shared is left over (the remainder).</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>20 sweets shared by 4</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>20 ÷ 4</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 5 each</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unequal shares</td><td>Every share must be the same.</td></tr><tr><td>Groups or each</td><td>Read what the question asks.</td></tr><tr><td>Left-overs</td><td>Say how many are left.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the Foundation Phase?",
   "a": "Grade R to Grade 3 in South African schools. Learners are about 7 to 8 years old in Grade 2."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the difference between sharing and grouping?",
   "a": "Sharing: how many each. Grouping: how many groups."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does ÷ mean?",
   "a": "Shared by or grouped into."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can there be a remainder?",
   "a": "Yes."
  }
 ],
